# Development Process (Gated PRs)

Every feature or fix follows the workflow in `docs/DEVELOPMENT_PROCESS.md` —
this rule is the PR-mechanics summary of that document, not a replacement.
Design ceremony is proportional to the change's risk-tier (see below); tests
are planned before production logic.

## Risk-Tier Policy

Every change is classified into a risk-tier before implementation —
**High** (four design artifacts), **Medium** (one concise design note;
the default for any non-High, non-Low change), or **Low** (design
artifacts exempt only). The **highest applicable tier wins**: a small
diff cannot be downgraded when it touches a high-tier path or
invariant. Record the chosen tier and rationale in the PR description.

- **High** (security/auth, agent identity or permissions, enforcement,
  migrations/schema/RLS, tenant or learning data, secrets or deployment
  boundaries, CI workflows, hooks; path globs include `src/*/security/**`,
  authentication and authorization middleware, agent-identity or scope
  dispatch, `enforcement/**`, `learning/tables.py` and its migrations,
  `.github/workflows/**`, `.cursor/hooks*`, deployment manifests, and
  secret configuration). High requires all four design artifacts before
  implementation: **architecture map**, **data-flow diagram**,
  **workflow states or wireframes**, and **threat model**. Primary
  layout: `docs/designs/<feature>/{ARCHITECTURE_MAP,DATA_FLOW,WORKFLOW_STATES,THREAT_MODEL}.md`.
- **Medium is the default** risk-tier for any non-High, non-Low change,
  including single-file behavior changes. Medium requires **one concise
  design note** covering architecture impact, data movement, failure
  behavior, risks, and planned tests.
- **Low** applies to documentation- or presentation-only work, test-only
  changes, and fixes under 20 gross changed lines measured as
  **additions plus deletions** (excluding mechanically generated
  artifacts) that do not touch high-tier paths or invariants. Low is
  **exempt from design artifacts** only. Branch isolation, CI,
  applicable tests, human ownership, and post-change review still
  apply.

## PR Mechanics

1. **Plan sign-off before code (proportional to risk-tier).** High
   changes get the four design artifacts reviewed by the relevant
   domain experts. Medium changes get one concise design note reviewed
   by the matching reviewer. Implementation starts only after every
   required reviewer returns APPROVED; CHANGES-REQUIRED verdicts are
   folded into the plan and re-reviewed. Low-tier changes may skip
   design ceremony but not branch isolation, CI, tests, ownership, or
   review.
2. **One focused PR per change, on a branch.** Never commit directly
   to `main`.
3. **Validation before review.** Whatever validation the project
   ships (`bash scripts/validate.sh`, `make test`, `pre-commit`,
   generated-project checks, etc.) must pass fully before requesting
   any review. Treat warnings as review items, not noise.
4. **Post-implementation review of the actual diff.** Bugbot plus the
   matching domain expert review the branch. Findings are fixed with
   regression tests (a test that fails on the pre-fix code), then
   re-reviewed. Nothing is pushed or merged without an explicit final
   APPROVED. Every confirmed finding is then classified by a human
   maintainer as either a one-off defect or a recurring bug class; a
   recurring-class fix cannot receive final approval until the same PR
   ships a regression test, an update to the nearest relevant agent
   rule or instruction, and a register entry in
   `docs/BUG_CLASS_REGISTER.md` linking the source finding, invariant,
   rule change, and regression test. Agents may propose entries but
   cannot auto-classify, self-edit their own rules, or approve their
   own rule changes. See `docs/DEVELOPMENT_PROCESS.md` for the full
   contract.
5. **Strongest model only.** Implementation and review subagents run
   on the strongest available model. If a subagent silently falls back
   to a weaker model, discard its output and redo it — or retro-audit
   anything already merged.

## Standing constraints

- **Detect, never auto-act:** shipped defaults may flag, log, or
  refuse explicitly — they never silently alter prompts, truncate
  listings, or degrade responses. Enforcement beyond flags is opt-in
  via env flags, default off.
- **Honest docs:** every capability claim cites code and tests;
  limitations go in GOVERNANCE Non-Claims. Never overclaim.
- **Schema changes** go through the `MIGRATIONS` mechanism in
  `learning/tables.py` with the frozen v1 baseline. Never add columns
  only to the live `TABLE_COLUMNS` baseline.
- **Line-cap headers are honest:** respect each module's stated cap;
  if a file must grow, update the header in the same PR rather than
  ignoring it.
